  "notes": [
    "Frozen judging corpus. Each entry is one moment mid-typing: `context` is text already committed, `prefix` is the partial word under the cursor. The full field content is `context + prefix`.",
    "`prefix` empty means the probe is next-word prediction, not completion.",
    "`intended` is present only where the typed characters are a mistake with one obvious target word. It is what a human would have meant, not a promise about what any engine returns.",
    "`mustNotCorrect` marks entries where changing the typed text on space is the failure. The repo's named example is `I` becoming `idea`.",
    "`acceptable` is human judgement about what a good keyboard would offer, authored from the `probes` line without reading any captured reference. It is not a capture and does not stand in for one. `acceptableIsClosed` says how to read it: true means the list is meant to be complete and offering something else in slot one is wrong; false means the slot is genuinely open and the list is a sample of good answers, never a whitelist. `acceptableSource` is on every entry that has one so it can never be mistaken for what iOS returned. Absent on the `intended` and `mustNotCorrect` entries, which already carry a stronger criterion.",
    "`keyboard` is the layout active while typing `prefix`, either `en_US` or `he_IL`. Code-switch entries switch layout mid-sentence, so their `keyboard` differs from the sentence's language.",
    "`language` is the sentence, not the layout: `en`, `he`, `he-en` (Hebrew sentence, Latin word), `en-he` (English sentence, Hebrew word).",
    "In code-switch entries a Hebrew clitic (ה, ב, ל) can sit at the end of `context` with the Latin `prefix` fused straight onto it, as in `צריך לסגור את ה` + `spri`. That is not a schema slip: it is where the layout actually changes mid-word, and it is exactly how the sequence gets typed.",
    "Nothing here records what the stock keyboard or any engine actually offered. Captured stock behaviour lives in reference/manifest.json."
  ],
